\FrontChapter{Preface}

Suppose you have three different files named \FileName{notes.txt}. The
search window can show you all three, but it may not tell you at a glance
which version has the passage you remember. At a terminal you can ask a
more precise question: look in this directory for this phrase and show
me the matching lines. You still need to decide which note to keep. The
terminal's job is to make the evidence easier to inspect.

That is the kind of work this book teaches. You do not need to write
software, administer a server or replace the graphical tools you already
use. The terminal is useful when you want to repeat an action, see what
happened, or hand someone else the exact instructions. A picture viewer
is still the right tool for deciding which of two photographs to frame.
The shell can help you find the two files without opening fifty others.

\section*{Where to start}

If the prompt is unfamiliar, start with Part I. It shows you where a
command runs, how to find things and how to make changes without
practicing on anything valuable. Part II shows how to read, compare and
reshape text, including data exported from other applications. Part III
explains why the shell sometimes changes what you typed before a command
sees it, and introduces scripts, processes and permissions. Part IV puts
those skills to work on daily tasks, remote access and backups.

If you came with a particular job in mind, read the beginning of its
chapter and follow the referenced earlier chapters when a term or
technique is unfamiliar. Use Appendix~\ref{app:portability} to check
which pieces are specific to a system or shell, and
Appendix~\ref{app:quick} as a reminder after you have tried them.

\section*{How to use the examples}

\BookConventions

Read the explanation, then type one example at a time and compare what
you got to what the example says to look for. A different home-directory
name is ordinary; a different destination for a copy is a reason to stop.
If a setup command fails, do not continue by guessing which directory
you are in. Check first. Do not type a line from an output box into the
terminal.

Check whether a task needs a particular shell or an optional tool before
running it. An example described as portable uses a standard interface;
that does not mean every tool is already installed on every machine.
Examples involving another computer require a machine and account you
are authorized to use. It is fine to study those examples without
connecting.

Use the practice data and try the exercises before looking at the
solutions. If a result differs, make one small change at a time. Record
the exact command and the part of the result that surprised you. Knowing
how to ask a more discriminating second question is a better measure of
progress than remembering a command's flags.
